  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nNortheast winds have gusted to around 15kts over the southeast\nhalf of the region already today, but they will diminish through\nthe afternoon and become light tonight. Guidance indicates that\na light breeze may persist over at least the eastern half of the\nregion tonight, which could limit radiational cooling to some \nextent. However, over the Ozark Foothills and our typically cool\nlocations in southern Illinois, where winds will likely be \ncalm, temperatures will drop down near the freezing mark for an \nhour or two. Still feel that frost will be the primary impact \nover most of the region, so will not make any changes to the \nheadline. However, will delay its beginning til 1 AM CDT to \nbetter match the onset of frost development.\n\nDry surface high pressure will be the rule through the remainder\nof the forecast. High pressure aloft will build overhead through\nTuesday and then remain in place into next weekend. The result\nwill be the ideal fall weather most of us really look forward\nto and have been waiting not so patiently for. Highs will climb\nback to normal in the lower 70s by Monday and then climb into \nthe middle and upper 70s for Wednesday through next Saturday. A \nfew readings of 80 or greater will be possible, especially over \nsoutheast Missouri. Low temperatures will remain chilly in the \nmid to upper 30s Sunday night/early Monday morning and some \nfrost cannot be ruled out in a few locations. Then lows will \ntrend upward through the 40s through the work week.",
